---
import Developers from "~/layouts/Developers.astro";

const nativeExample = `{
  "client_id": "https://shop.example/desktop/oauth-client-metadata.json",
  "client_name": "Example Shop for desktop",
  "application_type": "native",
  "redirect_uris": [
    "http://127.0.0.1/callback",
    "example.shop:/callback"
  ],
  "token_endpoint_auth_method": "none",
  "brigid_attributes": { "email": "optional" }
}`;

const ways = [
  [
    "The loopback address",
    "http://127.0.0.1/callback or http://[::1]/callback. The port is not compared: open any free one when the sign-in starts. Not localhost, which a machine can resolve elsewhere.",
  ],
  [
    "Your domain, reversed, as a scheme",
    "example.shop:/callback for shop.example. Being derived from your domain, no other application can claim it.",
  ],
  [
    "An https link on your domain",
    "https://shop.example/app/callback, opened by your application as a claimed link (App Links, Universal Links).",
  ],
];
---

<Developers
  title="Desktop and mobile apps · Developers"
  description="Sign in with brig·id from an application people install: a public client, the system's browser, PKCE, a document on your domain."
  lead="Desktop and mobile apps,"
  accent="no secret inside."
  intro="An installed application is a public client (RFC 8252): anything shipped inside it can be read, so it carries no secret and no key. PKCE protects the exchange; the system's browser protects the passkey."
>
  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>A document all the same</h2>
    <p>
      Publish a document on your domain, as a website does, with
      <code>application_type: "native"</code> and
      <code>token_endpoint_auth_method: "none"</code>. One domain can publish
      several documents: one for the website, one for each application.
    </p>
  </div>

  <pre class="code rise"><code>{nativeExample}</code></pre>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>Coming back to the application</h2>
    <p>Its redirect URIs are one of:</p>
  </div>
  <dl class="fields rise">
    {ways.map(([name, text]) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{name}</dt>
        <dd>{text}</dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <div class="prose rise">
    <h2>The system's browser, always</h2>
    <p>
      Open the sign-in in the browser of the system (or an in-app browser tab
      that is the system's: <code>ASWebAuthenticationSession</code>, Custom
      Tabs), never in a web view your application draws itself. The passkey
      ceremony needs the real address of the server, and the person needs to see
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>The rest is the website's flow</h2>
    <p>
      Find the person's server from the domain they type, run the Authorization
      Code flow with PKCE <code>S256</code>, exchange the code with your{" "}
      <code>client_id</code> and the verifier, check the ID token. Know the
      person by <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code> together. The details are
      on <a href="/developers/websites/">the website guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Without a browser</h2>
    <p>
      A command line or a television, with no browser to open, would use the
      device flow (RFC 8628). It is planned for after the first version.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="next rise">
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/websites/">
      Website guide
    </a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/developers/reference/">
      Reference
    </a>
  </div>
</Developers>
